{"id":"leetcode-152","version":1,"status":"approved","title":"Maximum Product Subarray","summary":"Given an integer array, identify a subarray with the highest product and provide that product.","statement":"Given an integer array, identify a subarray with the highest product and provide that product. Ensure the result fits within a 32-bit integer. Remember, a single-element array's product is the element itself.","constraints":["1–20000 integers from -10 to 10. Every contiguous subarray product fits signed 32-bit range.","Choose a nonempty contiguous subarray. Zero separates potential runs; a negative value can turn the smallest ending product into the largest."],"topics":["arrays","dynamic-programming"],"prerequisites":[],"signature":{"name":"maximum_product","parameters":[{"name":"nums","annotation":"list[int]","adapter":"raw","source_parameter":null}],"returns":"int","return_adapter":"raw","node_policy":"values","result_mode":"return","mutated_parameter":null,"immutable_parameters":[]},"starter_code":"def maximum_product(nums: list[int]) -> int:\n    # Write your solution here.\n    pass\n","reference_solution":"def maximum_product(nums):\n    highest = lowest = best = nums[0]\n    for index in range(1, len(nums)):\n        value = nums[index]\n        a, b = value * highest, value * lowest\n        highest = max(value, a, b)\n        lowest = min(value, a, b)\n        best = max(best, highest)\n    return best\n","brute_force_solution":null,"known_wrong_solutions":["def maximum_product(nums):\n    ending = best = nums[0]\n    for value in nums[1:]:\n        ending = max(value, ending * value)\n        best = max(best, ending)\n    return best\n","def maximum_product(nums):\n    nums = [value for value in nums if value != 0] or [0]\n    highest = lowest = best = nums[0]\n    for index in range(1, len(nums)):\n        value = nums[index]\n        a, b = value * highest, value * lowest\n        highest = max(value, a, b)\n        lowest = min(value, a, b)\n        best = max(best, highest)\n    return best\n"],"target_time_complexity":"O(n)","target_space_complexity":"O(1)","estimated_minutes":35,"rubric":{"algorithmic_insight":2,"prerequisites":1,"implementation":1,"performance_pressure":1,"edge_cases":0},"hints":["Multiplying by a negative number reverses the ordering of products.","Keep both the minimum and maximum product ending at the previous position.","At each value, consider starting anew or multiplying either previous extreme; update both extremes and the global best.","Compute both products before overwriting either previous extreme, and do not skip zeros."],"editorial":"Unlike maximum-sum subarrays, a product needs both extremes because a negative multiplier swaps their roles. Each position either starts a new nonempty subarray or extends a previous one. Keeping only the ending minimum and maximum is sufficient to describe the next extremes. The oracle explicitly enumerates every contiguous start/end interval and multiplies its values. All generated products stay within the source's signed-32-bit guarantee.","tests":[{"id":"lc-152-1","arguments":[[2,3,-2,4]],"expected":6,"comparator":"exact","visible":true,"explanation":"The contiguous pair [2,3] has product 6, better than the later products.","expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-152-2","arguments":[[-2,0,-1]],"expected":0,"comparator":"exact","visible":true,"explanation":"The maximum is 0. The two negative values cannot be joined across the intervening zero.","expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-152-3","arguments":[[0]],"expected":0,"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-152-4","arguments":[[1]],"expected":1,"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-152-5","arguments":[[-1]],"expected":-1,"comparator":"exact","visible":false,"explanation":null,"expected_node_order":null},{"id":"lc-152-6","arguments":[[-10]],"expected":-10,"comparator":"exact","visible":fa